# Opm Versioning Contract

File: ooda/opm/docs/versioning.oot

This document defines the version contract that opm uses for itself, for the parent ooda toolchain, and for every package it installs. The contract is fail-closed. If a check fails, opm refuses to run.

## 1. The VERSION File

`ooda/opm/VERSION` declares opm's own version and its requirements on the toolchain that hosts it. The format is `NAME=MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-PRERELEASE]`, one per line.

```
opm=0.7.0
ooda=2.10.0
ooda_max=2.99.0
oodac=2.10.0
std=2.10.0
api_surface=10
```

## 2. Field Semantics

- `opm` — opm's own version. Uses Semantic Versioning. Bump MAJOR on a breaking change to the CLI surface or the lock format. Bump MINOR for a backwards-compatible feature. Bump PATCH for a backwards-compatible fix.
- `ooda` — the minimum required ooda toolchain version. opm refuses to run if the installed ooda is older.
- `ooda_max` — the maximum compatible ooda version. Pinned to `2.x` because a 3.x toolchain implies a new contract.
- `oodac` — the minimum compiler version. Same scheme as `ooda`.
- `std` — the minimum standard library version. Same scheme.
- `api_surface` — a structural check. The loader asserts that the opm `ANCHOR.oo` exposes exactly this many public subdirs. If a new subdir is added or removed, the number changes and the contract is bumped.

## 3. Bump Rules

Bump the MAJOR part of `opm` when any of these change:

- A subcommand is removed or renamed.
- The `cli` argv shape changes.
- The lock format changes in an incompatible way (see section 6).
- The `ooda.pkg` manifest schema changes in an incompatible way.

Bump the MINOR part when:

- A new subcommand is added.
- A new flag is added to an existing subcommand.
- A new key is added to the lock.
- The `api_surface` count grows.

Bump the PATCH part when:

- A bug is fixed.
- The wording in a help line changes.
- A new `ooda_max` pin is added.

## 4. Range Syntax

opm supports the common range operators on package versions. The catalog resolver (`core/core.oo`) understands these forms:

- `=1.2.3` — exact match.
- `^1.2.3` — caret. Same MAJOR, version `>= 1.2.3` and `< 2.0.0`.
- `~1.2.3` — tilde. Same MAJOR.MINOR, version `>= 1.2.3` and `< 1.3.0`.
- `*` — any version.
- `>=1.2.3`, `<=1.2.3`, `>1.2.3`, `<1.2.3` — comparisons.
- Compound: `^1.2.3 ~1.2.0` — AND of all parts.

When no operator is given, opm treats the version as `=X.Y.Z` (exact pin).

## 5. Resolution

opm ships two resolvers:

- `pm_resolve` — single-pass. Walks the catalog once, picks the highest compatible version, stops on the first conflict. This is the default for `add`.
- `pm_pubgrub_resolve` — backtracking. Records conflicts as `(root set, bad version)` pairs, picks the next unsatisfied package from the queue, tries the highest compatible version, and backtracks to the next-lower on a transitive dep miss. This is opt-in with `--pubgrub`.

Both resolvers have a depth and step bound. A bad catalog cannot loop forever.

## 6. Lock File Format

The lock is at `.ooda_modules/lock`. It is a line file. The first line is the live pin header: `oodac=<sha>` and `std=<sha>`. Each following line is one of:

- `<name>|<version>|<algo>:<digest>` — a package line.
- `# <text>` — a comment.
- blank line — ignored on read.

The lock is rewritten on every successful install. The hash is sorted lexicographically before the write, so the same set always produces the same lock bytes. The lock is a v2 format; v1 is not supported.

## 7. Fail-Closed Behavior

opm refuses to run when any of the following is true:

- The installed ooda toolchain is below the declared `ooda` floor.
- The installed ooda toolchain is at or above `ooda_max`.
- The installed oodac is below `oodac`.
- The installed std is below `std`.
- The `api_surface` count does not match the actual `ANCHOR.oo` subdir count.
- A required subdir is missing or contains zero `.oo` files.
- The lock is malformed (any package line that does not parse).
- A `min_pin` declared on a manifest is below the running opm's `min_pin`.

There are no silent fallbacks and no compatibility shims. A version contract that does not hold is an error.

## 8. Breaking Changes

A breaking change is any change that requires a consumer to update their code. Examples:

- Removing or renaming a subcommand.
- Removing a field from the lock.
- Removing a `caps=` token from the default set.
- Changing the shape of the error output (scripts that grep the output will break).

Each breaking change bumps the MAJOR part of `opm`, the MAJOR part of `ooda_max`, and re-issues the contract. Consumers that pin `^0.7.0` will not pick up a `1.0.0` automatically. They must opt in.
